# Intent: a light tap on the head counts, and the strip's idle noise never makes a touch by itself (owner mission 2026-11-12, "IMU-assisted head tap"). The owner's light taps read only 1 on the strip, the level its idle noise reads on 200 to 400 samples a minute on a noisy day, so every rule so far either ignored them (080) or smiled at phantoms (076, 078). The motion sensor (BMI270) tells them apart: a tap jolts the head, noise does not. The IMU task now reads the sensor every 10 ms (it read every 100 ms and could not see a tap; the shake, orientation and stall checks keep their 100 ms rhythm on every tenth sample) and marks a jolt: one sample whose change from the previous one is at least 1.0 m/s^2 after 150 ms of stillness, and not within 0.6 s of a servo command (a head move of the unit's own can start with a jerk of 3 to 5; a longer blank swallowed taps while the host was centring the head). Measured 2026-11-12 on the unit: idle floor 0.1 with one 0.46 blip in ten minutes; ten light taps on the strip 1.1 to 4.6 (seven read strong, three read 1 for 2 to 4 samples and were lost before); fifteen light taps on the front shell 2.0 to 7.8; five desk slaps beside it 2.3 to 4.4; typing under 0.5; a pick-up starts under 0.5; a put-down is big but moving before. The strip feels the finger before the impact (capacitive), so the strip recogniser turns a contact at any level into a press when a jolt lands within 100 ms before or 150 ms after its first touched sample (the three weak-reading taps: -58, +70, +112 ms); a jolt makes at most one tap. A tap by the motion sensor alone ("front tap", a jolt of 2.0 with no strip contact) is built but off: a desk slap is the same size as a light tap on the shell. Residual, measured: on a noisy day a hard knock on the desk that lands inside a noise run's 250 ms window counts as a tap (two of five shell taps did so through noise); with nobody knocking there is nothing. Press events say strong=1 (an older host trusts it), tap=1, strip=1 or 0, the jolt size (m/s^2 times 100) and its distance from the first touched sample in ms; head_touch_stats counts taps, imu_stats counts jolts, the biggest, those blanked by a servo move and front taps. A serial trace for tuning runs for the first half hour after boot (I, IS, J, F, T, P lines).
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal_imu.cpp, hal_head_touch.cpp, hal_mcp.cpp, hal_servo.cpp (MIT). Applies after 080 with --unidiff-zero.
# Check after a flash: a light tap on the strip (the big grey area at the back of the head top) gives "unit touch: head_touch strong=1 tap=1 strip=1 jolt=N gap=M" at once and wakes a sleeping unit on its next look; a slow rub still gives presses with tap=0; typing beside it gives nothing; the unit's own head moves give imu_stats blanked>0 and no head_touch; ten untouched minutes with head_touch_stats weak high give presses=0 taps=0.
